ified, `TypeError: The argument 'stdio' is invalid`. The remaining choices are `'inherit'` (leaks into llame's own diagnostics), `'ignore'` (no diagnostics at all), or a file on disk (an unsanitized secret sink). It also lacks the teardown escalation the shutdown requirement needs.\n\nA llame-authored transport was the earlier recommendation and was rejected here on the original evaluation. It was costed as buying a bounded read buffer and process-group termination at the price of permanently diverging from upstream, with both gaps accepted instead — see the first two entries under Risks / Trade-offs as they stood at the time. The read-buffer half of that tradeoff is superseded by the revision above; process-group termination remains a real, accepted gap (see Risks / Trade-offs).\n\n`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`'s `StdioClientTransport` supplies what the specs require: a `stderr` accessor returning a stream created before `start()` so early output is not lost, a `pid` accessor, and a teardown ladder of `stdin.end()` → 2s → `SIGTERM` → 2s → `SIGKILL`. It is also what the AI SDK's own documentation recommends pairing with `createMCPClient`, and what Claude Code uses. One further difference matters for D2: the official transport merges as `{ ...getDefaultEnvironment(), ...serverParams.env }`, so a declared value overrides an inherited one, whereas `@ai-sdk/mcp`'s `getEnvironment` assigns the allowlist _after_ the caller's map and would silently clobber a declared `PATH`. Cost, as originally weighed here: promoting `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` from a transitive `shadcn` dependency to a direct pinned dependency of `apps/api`, and two MCP implementations in one process. `BoundedStdioTransport` keeps both of those costs unchanged — it still depends on the same pinned package for its exported message/environment helpers and still coexists with `@ai-sdk/mcp` in the same process — and adds no third implementation; it replaces the SDK's `StdioClientTransport` class specifically, 